# The documentation image is built from the repository root, because the API
# reference generator has to read `src/`. That makes everything below part of the
# build context by default — including a `node_modules` built for the developer's
# own operating system, which `COPY website/ ./` would then merge over the one
# `npm ci` just installed for the image.
#
# It is not only a size problem, though half a gigabyte of context is reason
# enough. It is that the resulting build depends on what happens to be on the
# machine that started it, which is exactly the CI-and-deploy divergence the
# single shared Dockerfile exists to prevent.

# Dependencies and build output — reinstalled and rebuilt inside the image.
**/node_modules
website/.next
website/out
website/.source
website/next-env.d.ts

# The generated API reference. Stage 1 writes it; a stale copy from the host
# would be overwritten anyway, but shipping it into the context invites the
# question of which one won.
website/content/api
website/.api-manifest.json

# Python build and tool state.
.venv
dist
build
**/__pycache__
**/*.egg-info
.mypy_cache
.pytest_cache
.ruff_cache
api-src/_build

# Version control and agent scratch space.
.git
.gitignore
.claude
.scratch
